AvbdMaterialTerms.cs, AvbdBodyEquation.cs, AvbdElasticConnectionMath.cs,
AvbdJointMath.cs, JointMath.cs, BodyRotation in PredictBodies.cs and
ApplyGyroscopicTorque.cs adapt the compliant ALM coefficients, force rows,
joint coordinates, rotation derivatives, angular inertia and gyroscopic
prediction from Newton.
The mathematical migration follows the project's research NewtonRigid.cpp.
AvbdJointMath.cs also retains a positive bound for force-weighted angular
geometric curvature, following the research BodyEquation.cpp addJointEquation.

JointMotorMath.cs, AvbdJointMotorMath.cs and XpbdJointMotorProjection.cs reuse
these common joint Jacobians and material coefficients for timed PD drives.

AvbdContactMath.cs adapts contactEquation and addContact from the research
NewtonRigid.cpp (Newton rigid VBD compliant contact, unilateral normal force,
disk friction curvature and approach damping). Contact multipliers are world
forces stored on persistent ECS contact entities; material combination and
normal restitution impulses use explicit C# stages and project parameters.

AvbdSupportedContactMath.cs reuses the same contact equations with the explicit
LRC auxiliary force in the total Coulomb projection, following the research
contactEquation extra-force handoff. LRC graph and support sources are listed
in ../LongRange/NOTICE.

CollisionGeometryMotion.cs and GeometryPairPrediction.cs adapt the research
Islands.cpp readCollisionExtent/findCollisionPairs and Bridge.cpp
readBodyTravel/readPredictedContacts motion-envelope and local-contact math.
The C# stages consume the common ECS substep start and predicted states,
project starting separation planes through weighted local body anchors,
and preserve their sample time separately from the contact response interval.

The C# implementation stores body equations and constraint multipliers in ECS
components, binds concrete constraint types through borrowed query rows and
uses the existing Job scheduler for its execution and completion.

